  "text": "Two numbers from the same dashboard, on the same morning. Total signups: up again, the way they are every single day. Users still cooking four weeks after joining: down two points. Only one of them can tell you something is wrong. Product sense is that judgment, and there's no code in this round. The numbers in this video all come from one made-up recipe app. Eleven questions get a figure, and three close the round in a sentence each. Begin with the number a whole team steers by.\n\nQuestion one. What is a north-star metric, and what makes a good one? It's the single number that best captures the value users get, the one every team pushes toward. A good one moves before revenue does, reflects real value, and is something the team's work can move. For this app: weekly cooks, people who open a recipe in cooking mode. Total signups is bigger, and says nothing about whether anyone cooks. Question two. What are guardrail metrics, and why do you need them? They're the health checks you refuse to harm on the way: crash rate, load time, unsubscribes, revenue. Almost any single number can be gamed. Send more notifications, and sessions climb while people quietly leave. Next, two ideas about proxies: when they move, and when they lie.",
